encircled with forest garlands and adorned with gem-encrusted ornaments;
descending with joy from the vehicle, [the Lord] came to the floor of the assembly. [54]

And they seated him upon an excellent jewel-lion-throne;
And Brahmā, Śakti, Śiva, and the others bowed to him with their heads,
All with folded hands, their limbs bristling with joy, their eyes filled with tears.
Smiling, he asked them about everything in a sweet voice;
Addressed [by them], the omniscient one began to speak:
O gods endowed with intellect, it is not fitting to speak [without understanding]:
For all living beings in the universe have power through the Śakti
By which the entire world, from Brahmā down to a blade of grass, is made of Prakṛti.
Truly, truly, without me—[and yet] by me—Śakti has been revealed.
And that goddess has appeared from me at creation by my will;
And she disappears into me at the end, during the dissolution of creation.
Prakṛti is the maker of creation and the supreme mother of all;
Equal to me, she is my Māyā, and therefore she is called Nārāyaṇī.
For a very long time, Śambhu performed austerities, meditating on me;
Therefore, she was given by me to him in the form of the fruit of his austerities.

I am the Self; Brahmā is the mind; Maheśvara is knowledge itself;
Viṣṇu himself is the five vital breaths; Prakṛti, the supreme goddess, is intellect.
Intelligence, sleep, and the like are all parts of Prakṛti;
And she is this daughter of the mountain king: so it is determined in the Veda.
I am the lord of Goloka and the eternal lord of Vaikuṇṭha;
Surrounded by cowherdesses and cowherds, there alone I have two arms myself;
Here, the lord of gods has four arms, is the lord of Lakṣmī, and is surrounded by attendants.
Higher and beyond Vaikuṇṭha, by five hundred million yojanas,
Is my dwelling in Goloka, where I am the husband of the cowherdesses.
The two-armed one is to be worshipped in the observance, and he is the giver of its fruit;
Whichever form one meditates upon, that very form gives that fruit.
O auspicious Śivā, complete your observance by giving Śiva as the priestly gift;
Giving a proper price again, you will receive your husband.
Just as cows are the body of Viṣṇu, so Śiva is the body of Viṣṇu;
Having given the price of cows to the twice-born, take your husband, O auspicious one!

You are action, You are the seed of action, the giver of the fruits of actions.
Yogins meditate upon that splendour of Yours which is bodiless;
Some, on the four-armed, tranquil, captivating beloved of Lakṣmī.
